ctic  structure they also have to be  representing the meaning of words  and the context in which they appear  that's a lot of it is discourse  structure so maybe just like the linear  projections in a transformer what we  should do is find a linear  transformation of these representations  which is just capturing syntactic  structure  so our goal will be to  make a linear projection linear  transformation projection of  these word vectors that gives us just  their syntax based representation and to  hope that in that subspace  distance in that subspace will  correspond to tree distance  and our goal will be to learn a single  linear transformation b  which makes that as true as possible  and so if we can do that  then we can recover syntax trees from  our bert representation simply by  projecting to that subspace and then  finding the minimum spanning tree  between the words in that subspace that  will be the syntactic structure of the  sentence  so to operationalize that we've trained  a burt model completely independently of  any knowledge of syntax just from  predicting words but then we make use of  a tree bank simply to try and find a b  matrix one b matrix used everywhere that  will do this projection onto the  syntactic subspace  and it turns out that if we do that we  can actually do it very well  so for simpler models such as word  embedding or older generation  recurrent neural networks they don't  actually learn that much syntax in  particular there's a baseline for  syntactic structure in english that  english has strongly write with  branching syntactic structure so if you  just attach each word to the previous  word you get about 49  and these models don't do much better  than that but the modern large language  pre-trained language models do much much  better so even an elmo model does pretty  well and if you go up to  a bert model you're actually doing a  pretty good job at recovering the  syntactic structure of sentences so  here's one example wher