very  procedure  for the grammar of the human language  indeed this was part of his argument  that actually the structure of human  languages has to be innate within human  brains but interestingly it seems that  with these modern large transformer  language models we've actually  made a mechanical discovery procedure  for human language syntax and that's  actually quite remarkable  okay let me move on now to mention one  other piece of work that i've been  involved in particularly with my student  um kevin clark and this is an  alternative approach to learning  large transformer language models that  is more efficient  and i've contextualized this by looking  at results on the glue benchmark which  is a benchmark for natural language  understanding tasks it has a variety of  different tasks in it but the commonest  kind of task is natural language  inference tasks and in these tasks  you're given two pieces of text a  premise piece of text and particular  hypothesis so a premise maybe of hills  and mountains are especially sanctified  in jainism and then a hypothesis jainism  hates nature and you have to say whether  the hypothesis is supported by the text  contradicts the text or they don't have  any particular relation so in this  example the hypothesis is contradicted  by the text  so for this task  people  started exploring with simpler neural  network models getting about 67  but then from 2017 forward for the last  four years um performance on this task  has zoomed as people built  initial models like elmo gpt and bert  base and then is zoomed even more as  people have built larger  pre-trained  transformer language models like excel  net roberta and albert indeed again at  this point  the best models are performing better  than human mechanical  turk workers on these tasks so that  looks like amazing performance over four  years a three times error reduction  however if we change the x-axis instead  to the compute required things don't  look quite so rosy um so simple neural  models in retrospect used almost no  compute elmo was built to be actually  quite computer fisher and whereas once  we started  out into the bert family either the bert  base or the larger bird size we were  using massively more compute so burt  large uses 60 times more compute than  elmo how much compute is that  well  but base is using  6 times 10 to the 19 flops and but large  is then using  2 times 10 to the 20 flops  but in retrospect that was only the  start because people then went on from  there and built models like excel net  and roberta and while roberta is using  16 times more compute than burt large  but why stop there  moving on to more recent models again  like albert and the now very popular t5  it's using about 10 times more compute  than roberta um so  if you the kind of person that knows  your  si abbreviations we're now up to 30  zetta flops  3 times 10 to the 22 flops  so what we are actually observing is  each successive model is using  exponentially more compute than the last  model and we're starting to pay an  extremely high price in the amount of  compute needed to make what are  increasingly minor  benefits in terms of performance and so  the question is can we do any better  than that  my colleague andrew ing likes the slogan  ai is the new electricity but after  examining these graphs maybe we should  go with a different slogan electricity  is the new ai  so i want to introduce a different idea  that  kevin developed that has a bit of  promise in these regards so up until now  there have been two main ways of doing  language model pre-training there's  simply at each point predicting the next  word so from the artist sold you  predicted the from the art of soul that  you predicted painting classic language  models and things like the gpt series  um then there was the idea of burt of  mass language model training where you  used a two-sided context and so these  actually worked better but they had a  cost which was in practice you mask only  about 15 of the words so one word and  seven and you're effectively only  learning for the words that you've  masked so you're only learning from a  seventh of the data  um so what kevin's idea was that we  could improve on this by coming up with  a different pre-training task of replace  token detection so we're going to  replace  some words let's say again 15 of the  words  though you can do more by words similar  in meaning and then what our job is  going to be is to predict which words  have been replaced so we're just then  predicting original or replaced and so  this gives us two wins firstly we're  learning from every word rather than  only  one seventh of the words and secondly  rather than having a huge soft max over  the entire vocabulary at the top here  which is itself very expensive to  compute we're only doing binary  classification  so formally how you do this is you do  have to generate these plausible  alternatives and we actually do that  with a small generator language model  which can itself be a small mass  language model and interestingly it  actually works better for this model to  be small so it's not too good and that's  also then good for being energy  efficient  so we're then going to simultaneously  train these two models so the generator  is trained with standard mass language  model loss and the discriminator is  trained by binary classification  and then once we've done the training of  the discriminator we just throw away the  generator and use the representations  and the discriminator that we call  electra so this picture makes it look  rather like a gan  model but it's unlike again because the  generator is being trained by standard  mle rather than adversarially  um but using this model we do actually  achieve  what we'd like so the only real progress  in large language model  models is if you can drive things up to  the top left corner so that you're able  to get better performance while using  less energy 