g that people have  managed to build as standalone models  for question answering or co-reference  resolution  so it's in this  sense that these new models act as a  foundation for whole fields  and they have wonderful breadth but also  they potentially have worries because of  their homogenization  you are using one model for everything  and indeed despite their great success  there are still lots of concerns that  people rightly should have about  foundation models the models are still  brittle they frequently seem to lack  common sense or even internal  consistency in what they produce  the models are trained on large amounts  of data scoop from the real world  and partly because of that and partly  because of the nature of these models  they can easily generate biased or  offensive or just non-factual content  they can also be used at scale to  deliberately astroturf disinformation  the sheer size of these models also  creates  barriers and worries they require large  amounts of resources and many of these  models aren't as open to researchers  across the community as previous  generations of machine learning models  and as a response to that at stanford we  thought it's important to develop and  investigate these models more thoroughly  in an open academic context and so we've  started work in producing infrastructure  so that we can make at least medium  scale foundation model training  accessible and reliable with tools that  lots of researchers can use and you can  check out our start on that in the  mistral code base  which is  stable code for tr for training large  language models that fits inside um the  hugging face transformers framework we  think it's important to get lots more  people involved in  studying these foundation models not  only computer scientists and not only  people and who work at a handful of huge  technology companies  but also looking at what people who are  lawyers economists ethicists  medical sciences lots and lots of people  have things to contribute in  exploring the range of impacts and the  range of uses of foundation models  and we're wanting to explore  many aspects of these models we just  recently  released the first hefty report on  foundation models that you can find on  archive and we're increasing education  with new courses being taught on  foundation models this year  there is still a huge amount to explore  as to how to do better data governance  how to guide policies around the use of  these models how to better make these  models available to a broader community  of people while not incurring great  risks  but you know i think it is important to  realize that these foundation models  really are opening uh phase change in  the way that we're building uh models  for machine learning and artificial  intelligence and it's still day one in  the exploration of these models and that  there's a huge amount of work that many  in the community can and should be doing  to help shape how these models are  explored and the way that they end up  okay thank you for that talk um here's  a few of the relevant papers that i've  been mentioning in the talk and it's  been great getting to  present this at the amazon machine  learning conference thank you
 

